\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Local marginals on the circle and three-sphere}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use either compact orbit and product reference probability of
\ref{fdip-010I}, with continuous gauge-invariant joint weight and orbit probability
#{\mu_{\mathcal O}}. Its scalar marginal density is a constant
#{w_*}. For additional continuous real site factors #{t_x}, put
#{z_x=\int t_x\,\extd\mu_{\mathcal O}}. The resulting marginal
density and partition integral are
##{w_{{\rm site},t}(\phi)=w_*\prod_x t_x(\phi_x),
  \qquad Z_t=w_*\prod_x z_x.}
When the original weight and all additional factors are nonnegative
and #{Z_t>0}, the normalized scalar probability is the product of the
site probabilities with densities #{t_x/z_x} relative to
#{\mu_{\mathcal O}}. For the exponential tilt
#{t_\eta(o)=\ue^{\eta\langle u,o\rangle}} at one site, with the
real pairing of the compact model, that site's mean satisfies
##{\|\langle\phi_x\rangle_\eta\|
 \le \min\{1,\ue^{2|\eta|\|u\|}-1\}.}}

\p{The orbit of #{U(1)} is the unit circle and the orbit of
#{SU(2)} is the unit sphere in #{\mathbb C^2}. Given two scalar
configurations, choose independently at each site a group element
carrying the first value to the second. The change of link variables
preserves product Haar, and joint invariance gives equality of the
two link integrals by \ref{fdip-010I}. Site factors multiply their
common value. Finite-product integration of these continuous
functions on compact spaces gives the product of the orbit
integrals. In the nonnegative case positive #{Z_t} forces each of
these factors and #{w_*} to be positive, so normalization gives
the site probabilities.}

\p{In both groups multiplication by minus the identity sends every
orbit point to its negative. Invariance of #{\mu_{\mathcal O}}
therefore gives barycenter zero. All orbit points have norm one.
The exponential and its positive orbit integral lie between
#{\ue^{-|\eta|\|u\|}} and #{\ue^{|\eta|\|u\|}}. The density
differs from one by at most #{\ue^{2|\eta|\|u\|}-1}, and integrating
the unit vector against this difference bounds the mean. Averaging
unit vectors with a probability also bounds its norm by one.}
